    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d563">

                <pb facs="0662" n="594" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg594"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72/3314½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-02-06">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">6 February, 1917</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I entirely agree with
                    this suggestion so far as Panama is concerned.<anchor n="93" xml:id="d563-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>In the case of Cuba I have this question in my mind: might not such action on her
                    part be used as an excuse by Germany for an early attack upon her, or the
                    seizure of a naval base there, on the pretext that Cuba was in effect hostile?
                    It might be to Germany’s advantage, in other words, to declare war on Cuba (or
                    make it without declaring it) before taking action against us. A base for her
                    submarines on this side the sea would be most convenient. What do you think?</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
